WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
In New Zealand, one-lane bridges
are a common enough thing

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.840
that tourists are warned about them.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.160
The rules are simple.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.200
Approach with care, go slowly,

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.250
and if there's someone
coming the other way

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:13.580
then sometimes there are signs
that'll tell you who has priority.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.860
There is an exception to that rule
in just a couple of places, though,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.400
which is that, regardless
of what those signs say...

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.190
[train horn]

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:23.613
always let the train go first.

00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:27.490
- Dunedin Railways started
operating in the 1990s

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.740
and we run a tourist train business.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.940
We cart about 80-90,000
passengers a year.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.180
The route is 154km return from Dunedin

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:40.810
and follows a river gorge
up through the Taieri River

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.880
up onto the Strath Taieri plain.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.545
The scenery is spectacular,
how they built it.

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:51.340
They started in 1879, it was
completed in 1891, all by hand.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:52.650
It is a challenging route.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.077
It's really up and down.

00:00:54.077 --> 00:00:57.280
- Just to be clear, this isn't a tram,
or a trolley bus, or light rail,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.760
this is an actual train
running on the street here,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.960
because that was a better option

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.670
than trying to build a second bridge
on a very quiet road.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.980
Technically, I guess this is just
a very strangely shaped level crossing?

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.463
And there are a few other
bridges like this around the world,

00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:12.920
but almost all of them have lights
and sirens and barriers

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.800
to keep cars separated
when there's a rain approaching.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:19.763
Here, you just take care
and listen out for the train horn.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.720
- When the road bridge
further up the river failed,

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.320
they made the rail bridge a road bridge.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:28.750
There used to be a lot more in the country.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.860
It was a cheap option,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.940
why build two bridges
when you can build one?

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:34.570
They were mainly built
in less populated areas

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.700
because trains and cars don't
usually combine very well(!)

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.720
so you wouldn't build them in the cities.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.150
But the cars just have
to follow the road rules.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.380
There's stop signs at
either end of the bridge

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.210
and you don't do line speed
going up onto a road-rail bridge,

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.720
you're doing 10kph, blowing the whistle
and all that sort of thing.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.870
Hindon Road-Rail Bridge
is really there only

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:58.790
to give access to two sheep stations,

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.580
so there's not that much traffic on it.
Rarely you come across a car anyway.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.470
The train is going at a speed
that it can stop pretty easily,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.480
and usually the cars have to back up.

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.670
We've never had a problem
on a road-rail bridge.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:11.173
Level crossings are worse.

